isaid is in a total isolation from  the tunisian people i cannot see any way  but one way that is going back to  constitution going back to democratic  institutions otherwise  we are going into a very dumpy uh i mean  road  in which we feel that our country will  not be able to secure food  to the people of the country to the  people of tunisia and i don't think that  mr kaisaid will be able to to deliver or  to govern in the way he wants to so he  will have to if there are tens of  thousands today tomorrow there will be  hundreds of thousands  america we appreciate your time and  speaking to us thanks very much indeed  that's the situation there in tunis  let's uh speak to  uh  to jamal shall now who's expert on this  really you've reported from tunisia many  many times including uh during the arab  spring um so jamal what's your sense of  what's happening here and how  representative is what we're seeing of  the national sentiment  i mean it's representative in the sense  that it shows there is widespread  opposition to the uh president uh and to  his decisions um obviously in  democracies the best form to judge  representation is through elections um  will point to the fact that he won  presidential elections but  those participating in the streets will  point to the fact that there was a  parliament which kai said decided to  suspend and that parliament came about  through elections and the vast majority  of the members of parliament are opposed  to uh the decisions that he made and  herein is the issue is  if kaisaid wants to turn to the streets  for legitimacy whilst he may have had  the upper hand on july 25th when he made  the announcements it's becoming  increasingly clear that he no longer has  that or is at least at risk of losing  the street um so therefore the the  choice is actually to go to the ballot  boxes what we're hearing from whether it  be another even other political parties  and even the trade unions um and members  of uh political parties wh